On Tue, 16 Dec 2008, Ray Dillinger wrote:
> David Ploog wrote:
>
>> ...I learned a huge amount about design from Nethack. I cannot say I
>> admire it anymore, though: the design seems too erratic in many places. 
>> For example, lots of little details added in the game are made useless 
>> by other features.
>
> ie, the game was balanced but then somebody had an idea they thought
> was so nifty they had to put it in.... Unihorn healing, alchemy,
> etc. are cruft (and somewhat detrimental cruft) added to the simpler,
> better, more balanced game that nethack contains.

Yes. And by adding new features, old parts of the game may become 
underpowered or even useless (this is called "powergaming spiral", a
term I've first seen used by David Damerell).

> I am a fan of the idea that there should be more than one way to do
> something.  But there are several things which I think are too powerful
> and ought to be nerfed, or at least made much more difficult to obtain.

Yes. Several ways to do something is great. Nethack does this nicely
with crossing water: jumping, boulders, levitating, polymorphing etc.
However, one way that is strictly better than the rest is not good. (In
Crawl, we call these "no brainers" and try to kill them.) The unihorn
could be blamed, but it would be simple to fix it. I recall the idea of
giving it charges; it was mentioned several times in this group. This
might not be a perfect solution as you'll find a great number of
unihorns. A more radical solution would be to make a unihorn usable only
once. Anyway, I would have expected that the OP discusses questions like
these.

>> Cannot say anything about the code. But I know that Crawl was written
>> because the original authour wanted to avoid some deficits of Angband
>> and Nethack. (In Nethack, some of these are: permanent resistances
>> from eating; identical ascension kits; broken features like Elbereth
>> and genocide.)
>
> Yeah.  Identical ascension kits bother me too, although it's only
> one symptom of the problem that I focus on.
...
> This is much worse in Angband than it is in Nethack, but it definitely
> is there. Nethack's artifact limit does help to avoid this, BTW, but
> people who are then allowed to wish for the artifact of their choice
> (and most nethack characters do) tend to wind up similarly kitted.

I think that wishing is part of the problem. You are guaranteed four 
wishes, which is generally enough to fully equip yourself for the rest
of the game. I think that wishes should be abolished, or made much more
rare, or be more fuzzy. Sporkhack attacked this, of course, although I
don't recall in which way.

> Crawl IMO does a much better job of avoiding thousanduplet syndrome,
> largely because of the design of its skill set.

Another one is the absence of wishing and the absence of "optimal"
items, too.

>> Nethack certainly has strengths: the starting pet (and their behaviour)
>> is one, the shops are another. When I was a Nethack player, I thought
>> that the extreme care about minute details (TDTTOE) is awesome, but I
>> am not convinced anymore.
>
> The extreme care about minute details is awe-inspiring, but it is also
> a bit too predictable (and often too much to the player's advantage) to
> be always good.  I have to say I admire it, just because of the sheer
> work it took, and I think it's the kind of thing that's important for
> a game's codebase (or anyway one targeted at this niche, which mine is) to
> support.  But yes, it needs to be part of game design that's there for
> a reason, and right now it mostly seems like it was added because someone
> thought it was cool.  Perhaps better design would make the outcomes more
> random and ambiguous.

Yes. It seems as if new roguelikes were more conceptual than old ones.
(I've seen design documents for Sporkhack and Incursion, and I know that
POWDER and Steamband have basic design principles, too.)

>> I think that a game should challenge its
>> master players, too, but that's a different design principle.
>
> It's a much more difficult design principle to achieve, particularly
> when the code is publically released and people can make arbitrarily
> precise spoilers.  I agree that it's the ideal, but I'm not holding
> out for it because bluntly I think it's just plain too hard to do.

Well, the idea should be providing situations where spoilers don't
apply. If both melee and casting are viable, then the spoiler may delve
into excessive detail but the decision is left to the player. Once he
made that choice, however, there is opportunity for player errors (by
going against the former choices). Then again, special situations may
demand changing strategies... I think the ultimate goal should be to
provide many valid choices.

In Crawl, there are two other means of providing long term motivation:
(1) Side branches not needed for winning.
(2) Different combinations of race, class, god. Many of these play
really different and some are truly challenging.

David


